WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.062
After identifying a quality problem, we still need a response.

00:00:04.062 --> 00:00:06.502
Sometimes we can correct a value.

00:00:06.502 --> 00:00:13.422
Sometimes we retain it. Sometimes we exclude it from one analysis without deleting it from the source.

00:00:13.422 --> 00:00:16.742
Sometimes we impute missing information.

00:00:16.742 --> 00:00:23.237
Often, a quality flag is the most transparent choice when uncertainty remains.

00:00:23.237 --> 00:00:26.772
The objective is not to make the dataset look perfect.

00:00:26.772 --> 00:00:30.217
It is to make limitations explicit and manageable.

00:00:30.217 --> 00:00:34.662
In practice, quality rules should be tied to a use case.

00:00:34.662 --> 00:00:41.352
A fifteen-minute delay may be acceptable for a monthly report but unacceptable for a real-time alert.

00:00:41.352 --> 00:00:49.687
Likewise, an unusual physiological value should often be flagged and investigated rather than automatically

00:00:49.687 --> 00:00:53.748
deleted. Good cleaning preserves evidence and documents uncertainty.
